:{}}},app:{baseURL:"/",buildId:"cc78f444-4b49-472b-99f1-c71736000415",buildAssetsDir:"/_nuxt/",cdnURL:""}}</script><script type="application/json" data-nuxt-data="nuxt-app" data-ssr="true" id="__NUXT_DATA__">[["ShallowReactive",1],{"data":2,"state":3668,"once":3688,"_errors":3689,"serverRendered":892,"path":3691,"pinia":3692},["ShallowReactive",3],{"is-admin":4,"posts":5,"ladder:daily:2026-09-27:live":2419},false,[6,902,1765],{"id":7,"title":8,"author":9,"body":13,"canonical":886,"category":887,"date":888,"description":889,"extension":890,"feedback":886,"meta":891,"navigation":892,"ogImage":893,"path":897,"seo":898,"sitemap":899,"stem":900,"thumbnail":896,"tool_slug":886,"unsponsored":4,"updated":886,"__hash__":901},"blog\u002Fblog\u002Fhtml-css-to-image-review.md","HTML\u002FCSS to Image Review - URL Screenshots, Social Cards and MCP",{"name":10,"avatar":11,"url":12},"Thomas Sanlis","https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Favatars\u002Fthomas_sanlis.jpg","https:\u002F\u002Fthomas-sanlis.com",{"type":14,"value":15,"toc":869},"minimark",[16,27,30,33,45,50,55,58,61,85,89,101,115,119,123,126,129,132,147,151,156,159,181,188,192,215,218,222,239,266,270,273,290,294,336,345,358,370,384,399,403,410,414,421,439,443,449,460,464,468,483,489,511,518,521,524,561,578,582,632,636,639,734,737,743,746,750,767,774,778,781,785,791,796,825,830,863],[17,18,19,20,26],"p",{},"Welcome to this ",[21,22,25],"a",{"href":23,"rel":24},"https:\u002F\u002Fhtmlcsstoimage.com\u002F?ref=uneed.best","sponsored noopener","HTML\u002FCSS to Image"," review ✨",[17,28,29],{},"Every product I've built eventually needs to turn something into a picture. A social card for each blog post. A screenshot of a customer's page inside a report. A shareable \"you ranked #1 today\" graphic. And every time, the same wall: you need a real browser, running somewhere, with the right fonts installed, waiting the right number of milliseconds before it clicks the shutter. Doing that yourself means a Chromium binary in your deploy, a memory ceiling you'll hit on a Tuesday, and a font that renders correctly on your Mac and as Times New Roman in production.",[17,31,32],{},"HTML\u002FCSS to Image (HCTI to its users) has been selling the way out of that since 2018. It's run by Code Happy, LLC, and by their own count more than 100 million images have gone through it. You POST HTML and CSS, or a URL, or a saved template plus some values, and you get back a permanent, CDN-cached image URL.",[17,34,35,36,40,41,44],{},"Which means the interesting question for a review in 2026 isn't whether the core works. It's what they've built on top of it. So the founder pointed me at the two newest pieces: the visual ",[37,38,39],"strong",{},"Template Editor",", and the hosted ",[37,42,43],{},"MCP server"," that lets an AI assistant render images on your account. I tested both hands-on, plus the html-to-image and URL screenshot endpoints underneath them. I started on the free plan, and part-way through the founder bumped the account to a paid allowance, which is how I was also able to test PDFs, batching and the Open Graph configuration. I'll say which tier each limit belongs to as I go.",[46,47],"custom-image",{"src":48,"caption":49},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F1.png","The HTML\u002FCSS to Image home page.",[51,52,54],"h2",{"id":53},"getting-in","Getting in",[17,56,57],{},"Signup is free and the free plan is real: 50 images a month, no card.",[17,59,60],{},"Once you're through, the dashboard is open and an API key already exists, created the moment you picked a plan. Auth is HTTP Basic: the API ID is the username, the key is the password. The Overview page shows your current billing window (mine read \"Sep 10 to Oct 10\") against your allowance, plus a historical usage chart you can flip between hourly, daily, monthly and per billing period.",[17,62,63,64,67,68,72,73,76,77,80,81,84],{},"The nicest thing in the dashboard is the ",[37,65,66],{},"Image Console",". It's a two-pane editor with HTML, CSS and Options tabs on the left, and on the right three tabs: ",[69,70,71],"em",{},"Live preview",", ",[69,74,75],{},"Rendered image",", and ",[69,78,79],{},"Sample request",". The live preview renders in your own browser, instantly, and costs you nothing. Only when you click ",[69,82,83],{},"Generate image"," does it hit the API and spend a credit. That distinction sounds small until you're iterating on padding for the twentieth time.",[46,86],{"src":87,"caption":88},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F2.png","The Image Console: CSS on the left with colour swatches, live in-browser preview on the right.",[17,90,91,92,96,97,100],{},"The Options tab is where the real render controls live: light or dark colour scheme, ",[93,94,95],"code",{},"screen"," or ",[93,98,99],{},"print"," media type, and a full IANA timezone picker, which matters more than you'd think if your HTML prints a date.",[17,102,103,104,106,107,110,111,114],{},"Then the ",[69,105,79],{}," tab hands you working code for the exact request you just made. The console generates seven languages (TypeScript, C#, Python, PHP, Ruby, Go, cURL), and for five of them you pick a flavour: their official client library, or a direct API call with ",[93,108,109],{},"fetch"," or the language's equivalent. The TypeScript version came out using ",[93,112,113],{},"HtmlCssToImageClient.fromEnv()",", which reads the key from the environment instead of printing it into the snippet.",[46,116],{"src":117,"caption":118},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F3.png","Sample request generation: seven languages, with an official-client or direct-API variant for most.",[51,120,122],{"id":121},"the-template-editor","The Template Editor",[17,124,125],{},"This is the piece the founder most wanted looked at, and it's the most substantial thing HCTI has shipped in a while. A template is a saved layout with variables; you render it by POSTing values. Until now the only way to build one was to POST HTML with Handlebars placeholders, which still works and is still the right path if your templates live in version control. What is new is that you can also build one visually.",[17,127,128],{},"The editor gives you eight block types: Text, Image, Shape, raw HTML, and four panels (Free, Flex, Grid, Table). The canvas defaults to 1200 x 640 and there's a searchable preset picker with 29 sizes across three groups: social content, digital ads, and web\u002Fproduct\u002Fdocs. Each preset carries its dimensions, aspect ratio, a one-line note on when to use it, and a citation for where the number came from. Picking \"Open Graph Social Share\" set my canvas to 1200 x 630 and labelled it \"Cross-platform link preview default for websites and docs. Open Graph standard.\" That's a level of care I did not expect from a size dropdown.",[17,130,131],{},"Backgrounds do solid, gradient (linear, radial or conic, with an angle field and a draggable multi-stop bar) or pattern. Everything updates on the canvas as you type.",[17,133,134,135,138,139,142,143,146],{},"The text blocks are where it gets opinionated, and correctly so. There are two kinds, and the palette tells you which is which: a ",[37,136,137],{},"Rich Text Box"," (\"multi-line content with varied typography\", tagged HANDLEBARS TEMPLATING) and a ",[37,140,141],{},"Simple Text Box"," (\"single style text with auto-sizing support\", tagged DIRECT TEMPLATING). The simple one has ",[69,144,145],{},"Automatically Scale Font Size"," on by default with a min and a max, which is exactly the feature templated social cards need: your headline is 17 characters one day and 66 the next, and you want it to fit either way rather than clip.",[46,148],{"src":149,"caption":150},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F4.png","The Template Editor: canvas on the left, the Variables tab with a live test payload on the right.",[152,153,155],"h3",{"id":154},"variables-are-the-good-part","Variables are the good part",[17,157,158],{},"Almost every property in the panel has a small icon next to it, and clicking it turns that property into a template variable. Not just the text content: the font colour, the line height, the letter spacing, the min and max size. And you choose how a missing value behaves, from three options spelled out in plain English:",[160,161,162,169,175],"ul",{},[163,164,165,168],"li",{},[37,166,167],{},"Required",": the value can only come from a variable, and generation fails if it's missing.",[163,170,171,174],{},[37,172,173],{},"Ignored",": if it's absent from the payload, the property is simply unset in the resulting HTML.",[163,176,177,180],{},[37,178,179],{},"Fallback",": if it's absent, the property uses the value you left in the editor.",[17,182,183,184,187],{},"The variable key accepts dot notation (",[93,185,186],{},"profile.name","), so you can pass a nested object straight through.",[46,189],{"src":190,"caption":191},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F5.png","Binding a property to a variable: Required, Ignored or Fallback, with a dot-notation key.",[17,193,194,195,198,199,202,203,206,207,210,211,214],{},"I built an Open Graph card for a Uneed launch: a static eyebrow, a ",[93,196,197],{},"headline"," bound as Required, and a ",[93,200,201],{},"subline"," bound as Fallback. The Variables tab then collected both automatically, tagged them (STRING, PROPERTY, ACTIVE) and gave me a ",[37,204,205],{},"test payload"," panel with a ",[69,208,209],{},"Structured"," form view and a ",[69,212,213],{},"Raw JSON"," view showing the exact object I'd send to the API. Flip the ENABLED toggle and the canvas renders with those values. There's a \"Pop out\" button for the JSON editor and a key\u002Fsources table underneath.",[17,216,217],{},"Then I saved it and rendered it through the API, and it came back matching the canvas pixel for pixel. That sounds like the minimum bar, but visual editors sitting on top of a headless-browser renderer fail it constantly, and this one didn't.",[46,219],{"src":220,"caption":221},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F6.png","The saved template rendered through the API at 1200 x 630, matching the editor canvas exactly.",[17,223,224,225,227,228,231,232,235,236,238],{},"The variable modes are enforced where they should be, on the server. Omitting ",[93,226,197],{}," returned ",[93,229,230],{},"400"," with ",[93,233,234],{},"Template value 'headline' is required.",", and cost no credit. Omitting ",[93,237,201],{}," rendered fine and dropped in the editor's own copy. And a deliberately long 66-character headline shrank and wrapped to three lines inside its box, which is auto-scaling doing its job.",[17,240,241,242,245,246,249,250,253,254,257,258,261,262,265],{},"One thing to know before you wire it into anything: editor templates are stored as blocks, not markup. ",[93,243,244],{},"GET \u002Fv1\u002Ftemplate"," returns ",[93,247,248],{},"template_type: \"blocks\""," with a ",[93,251,252],{},"block_counts"," object and a structured ",[93,255,256],{},"variables"," array, and the ",[93,259,260],{},"html"," and ",[93,263,264],{},"css"," fields come back empty. Storing structure rather than a flattened string is the sane choice, but if you have code reading markup out of that endpoint, editor templates will look blank to it.",[51,267,269],{"id":268},"the-mcp-server","The MCP server",[17,271,272],{},"The other thing the founder asked me to try, and the part I found most quietly impressive.",[17,274,275,276,279,280,72,283,72,286,289],{},"The server lives at ",[93,277,278],{},"mcp.hcti.io"," over Streamable HTTP, and it does OAuth properly: dynamic client registration, PKCE with S256, three declared scopes (",[93,281,282],{},"image:create",[93,284,285],{},"template:read",[93,287,288],{},"template:create","). I registered a client and walked the flow by hand to see what it actually does, and the consent screen is the payoff. It names the client, shows the client ID and redirect URI, and translates the scopes into three lines a human can read: \"Create Images\", \"List Your Templates\", \"Create & Update Your Templates\". No API key gets pasted into an assistant, and the token I got back was good for 12 hours with a refresh token alongside it.",[46,291],{"src":292,"caption":293},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F7.png","The MCP authorization screen: scopes translated into plain English before you approve.",[17,295,296,297,72,300,72,303,72,306,309,310,72,313,72,316,72,319,72,322,72,325,328,329,261,332,335],{},"Connected, the server identifies itself as \"HTML\u002FCSS to Image API\" v1.0.2 and exposes 12 tools, 5 documentation resources (layout recipes, image authoring, parameter decisions, template authoring, troubleshooting) and 5 prompts. Alongside the render tools (",[93,298,299],{},"create_image",[93,301,302],{},"create_url_image",[93,304,305],{},"create_templated_image",[93,307,308],{},"create_batch_images",") there are the management ones (",[93,311,312],{},"create_template",[93,314,315],{},"update_template",[93,317,318],{},"list_templates",[93,320,321],{},"list_template_versions",[93,323,324],{},"list_storage_destinations",[93,326,327],{},"list_proxies",") and, sensibly, ",[93,330,331],{},"check_usage",[93,333,334],{},"get_max_batch_size",".",[17,337,338,340,341,344],{},[93,339,331],{}," is the one to run first, because it proves the connection without spending anything. Mine returned ",[93,342,343],{},"{\"images_used\": 2, \"images_allowed\": 50, \"overages_enabled\": false, \"next_reset\": \"2026-10-10T12:33:03Z\"}"," as structured content, not prose an assistant has to parse.",[17,346,347,348,350,351,353,354,357],{},"What sold me, though, was the writing. The server ships 1,459 characters of instructions to the client, and they read like someone who has actually watched agents misuse an image API. Call ",[93,349,334],{}," before building a batch. Resolve a template by name through ",[93,352,318],{}," and ask the user when no candidate is unambiguous. Leave ",[93,355,356],{},"include_image_data"," off unless a preview is genuinely needed. And this, which I've not seen a vendor say out loud: when inline image data comes back, inspect it for clipping, overflow, stray whitespace and illegible text, and if it's visibly wrong, fix the HTML and render again.",[17,359,360,361,363,364,366,367,369],{},"The error messages are built the same way. Calling ",[93,362,318],{}," without arguments returns \"Missing required argument 'count'. Call 'list_templates' again with a top-level 'count' value matching the tool's input schema.\" Plan gates come back as real refusals with reasons: ",[93,365,334],{}," on the free plan returned \"This plan does not support batch create\", and ",[93,368,327],{}," returned \"Your plan does not support proxies.\" An agent can act on all of that without a human in the loop.",[17,371,372,373,375,376,379,380,383],{},"I rendered both a card and a URL screenshot through MCP end to end. Each call returned in about 0.6 seconds with a markdown image link ready to paste, and the template listing came back with the full ",[93,374,256],{}," array including each mode, so an assistant can inspect a template's contract before rendering it. Install instructions are published for 11 clients, from Claude and Codex through Cursor, VS Code, Zed and JetBrains, plus Zapier and Pipedream. And the dashboard has an ",[37,377,378],{},"MCP Sessions"," page listing every grant with its client name, redirect URIs, and the IP, location and user agent captured at both registration and approval, each with a ",[69,381,382],{},"Disable"," button. For a credential that lets an AI spend your image quota, being able to see who claimed it and from where matters more than it would for a normal API key.",[17,385,386,387,390,391,394,395,398],{},"To see the whole thing behave, I wired the server into Claude Code (one ",[93,388,389],{},"claude mcp add --transport http"," line, project-scoped) and asked in plain English for a 1200x630 launch card with a gradient and an eyebrow. It came back with a hosted URL in one turn, and the interesting part is what I never specified: it chose ",[93,392,393],{},"selector"," to crop to the fixed-size element and loaded Inter through ",[93,396,397],{},"google_fonts"," on its own, because the tool schema tells it to give a fixed-size render an explicit element and the instructions tell it to request only the fonts the markup uses. That's the difference between an MCP server that exposes an API and one that has been taught how the API should be used.",[46,400],{"src":401,"caption":402},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F8.png","Claude Code connected to the HCTI MCP server, rendering a launch card from a one-line prompt and returning a hosted URL.",[17,404,405,406,409],{},"One gap worth naming: the tool arguments nest under a ",[93,407,408],{},"content"," object, and the example prompts in the docs read as though the parameters are flat. An assistant recovers instantly from the error message, but anyone hand-writing a client will guess wrong first.",[51,411,413],{"id":412},"dynamic-open-graph-images","Dynamic Open Graph images",[17,415,416,417,420],{},"This is the feature that answers \"I have 400 blog posts and none of them have a card.\" You add one ",[93,418,419],{},"og:image"," URL pattern containing the page path, HCTI opens that page, reads its meta tags, and returns the right image, cached until the inputs change.",[17,422,423,424,261,427,430,431,434,435,438],{},"The configuration screen is more considered than I expected. You give the config a name and a website origin (secure origin only, no path), and there are two checkboxes: whether the config is enabled at all, and whether HCTI should parse ",[93,425,426],{},"hcti:",[93,428,429],{},"html:tv:"," meta tags from each requested path. Then a slider for how long HCTI may reuse its last metadata check before asking the page again, from a 30-minute minimum to a year, with a note that your own origin cache headers can extend it. There's a collapsed \"Authentication and redirects\" panel for pages behind a token or cookie. And then the choice that matters: ",[37,432,433],{},"Page Screenshot",", which captures the page at the requested path with a page-only CSS override you can apply just before capture, or ",[37,436,437],{},"Template Values",", which reads values from the page and drops them into a template you already designed, with an explicit \"map page metadata to template fields\" table.",[46,440],{"src":441,"caption":442},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F9.png","Configuring dynamic OG images: origin, metadata refresh window from 30 minutes to a year, and a choice between capturing the page or filling a template.",[17,444,445,446,448],{},"The split is the useful part: the layout lives in one place you maintain, and each page supplies only its own values. I walked the whole configuration through but stopped short of pointing a live site's ",[93,447,419],{}," at it, since that means editing the head of a production page, so I can't personally vouch for the refresh-and-reuse behaviour over days. Everything up to that point is exactly what you'd want.",[17,450,451,452,455,456,459],{},"The free companion to it is worth knowing about even if you never sign up: the ",[37,453,454],{},"Social Card Previewer",", no account needed. Paste a public URL, pick one of 17 platforms, and it renders the unfurl inside a mock of that app while auditing the tags. I ran the DATA HIT review from this blog through it as Slack: it drew the card inside a fake ",[93,457,458],{},"#product-launches"," channel, then flagged the title at 54 characters as \"Good length for most previews\" and the description at 161 characters in amber with \"Some platforms may shorten this. Aim for 160 characters or fewer.\" It counted 11 metadata tags and offered the full report. That's a better OG debugger than most of the standalone ones.",[46,461],{"src":462,"caption":463},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002F10.png","The free Social Card Previewer: a Slack-shaped unfurl on the left, a per-field audit with character counts on the right.",[51,465,467],{"id":466},"the-part-that-has-been-around-a-while","The part that has been around a while",[17,469,470,471,231,474,261,476,478,479,482],{},"Under all of it sits the original API, and it is fast and unfussy. ",[93,472,473],{},"POST https:\u002F\u002Fhcti.io\u002Fv1\u002Fimage",[93,475,260],{},[93,477,264],{},", or with ",[93,480,481],{},"url",", and you get back an id and a permanent URL. Every POST I made returned in 0.5 to 0.9 seconds round trip from Europe, which is network rather than rendering: the vendor's own server-side timings are an order of magnitude lower, and the render itself happens on the first GET, which took 1.1 seconds for a card and 4.5 seconds for a URL screenshot. Output is PNG by default, and you switch to JPG, WebP or PDF just by changing the extension on the URL you already have.",[17,484,485,486,488],{},"The defaults are chosen well. HTML and template renders use a device scale of 2, so my 1200 x 630 card came back as a 2400 x 1260 file ready for retina, while URL screenshots use a scale of 1, because you asked for a screenshot of a page and not a poster. Google Fonts load with a single ",[93,487,397],{}," parameter and my DM Serif Display headline came out correct; emoji render in full colour through Twemoji unless you turn it off.",[17,490,491,492,495,496,499,500,502,503,506,507,510],{},"I captured Uneed's own pricing page at 1440 x 900, a JavaScript-driven Nuxt page, and it came back accurate down to the chat bubble in the corner, so the wait-for-load logic is doing real work. When it isn't enough there are the usual levers plus one I like: ",[93,493,494],{},"render_when_ready",", which waits for the page to call ",[93,497,498],{},"ScreenshotReady()"," itself. ",[93,501,393],{}," crops to a CSS selector, ",[93,504,505],{},"block_consent_banners"," kills cookie walls, and ",[93,508,509],{},"full_screen"," takes the whole scroll height.",[17,512,513,514,517],{},"The feature I'd not have thought to look for is ",[37,515,516],{},"deduplication",". I sent one card, then a URL screenshot, then re-sent the card's exact html and css: the third call came back with the same image id as the first, and the usage endpoint showed two credits for three POSTs. The duplicate was free. On the free and Basic plans the reuse window is up to a month, Pro lets you choose up to six, Scale up to a year.",[17,519,520],{},"That window is the whole cost model if you generate OG images lazily. Say 500 posts and 60,000 monthly page views, with the card rendered on request: without dedup you bill 60,000 renders and need the $249 Pro tier, and with a one-month window you bill roughly 500 and the $14 Basic tier covers it. Same code, one parameter apart.",[17,522,523],{},"Resizing and cropping also work off the finished URL rather than through a re-render, which means changing your mind about a crop costs nothing.",[17,525,526,529,530,533,534,537,538,72,541,544,545,548,549,552,553,556,557,560],{},[37,527,528],{},"PDFs"," are the same endpoint with ",[93,531,532],{},"format: \"pdf\"",", and they're real vector PDFs rather than an image in a wrapper. I rendered an invoice table at A4 with 20mm margins and printed backgrounds and got back a 34 KB one-page PDF with the table borders and header fill intact. One warning from my own mistake: ",[93,535,536],{},"pdf_options"," takes ",[93,539,540],{},"page_width",[93,542,543],{},"page_height"," and a four-value ",[93,546,547],{},"margins"," array, and if you invent plausible-sounding keys instead (I tried ",[93,550,551],{},"page_size"," and a single ",[93,554,555],{},"margin"," string) the API returns ",[93,558,559],{},"200"," and silently applies the defaults rather than telling you the keys are wrong. Check your first PDF against a ruler.",[17,562,563,566,567,249,570,573,574,577],{},[37,564,565],{},"Batching"," is ",[93,568,569],{},"POST \u002Fv1\u002Fimage\u002Fbatch",[93,571,572],{},"default_options"," object plus a ",[93,575,576],{},"variations"," array that overrides it per image. Three cards sharing a stylesheet came back in one request in 2.0 seconds. The ceiling is 25 on Basic and Pro, 100 on Scale.",[51,579,581],{"id":580},"everything-else-worth-knowing","Everything else worth knowing",[160,583,584,590,599,605,623],{},[163,585,586,589],{},[37,587,588],{},"Signed image URLs",", which put a whole render request inside the image URL itself.",[163,591,592,261,595,598],{},[37,593,594],{},"S3-compatible storage",[37,596,597],{},"proxy support"," from the 10,000 images\u002Fmonth tier up, with the option to switch HCTI's own storage and serving off entirely.",[163,600,601,604],{},[37,602,603],{},"Client libraries"," for five languages, docs examples for fourteen (Elixir, Rust, Kotlin and Google Apps Script among them), and Zapier, Make and GitHub Actions integrations.",[163,606,607,610,611,614,615,618,619,622],{},[37,608,609],{},"Operational endpoints"," you want and don't always get: ",[93,612,613],{},"GET \u002Fv1\u002Fusage"," rolled up by hour, day, month and billing period, ",[93,616,617],{},"GET \u002Fv1\u002Fimages"," with pagination, and ",[93,620,621],{},"DELETE"," on an image id that clears the CDN with it.",[163,624,625,631],{},[37,626,627,628],{},"A published OpenAPI spec and an ",[93,629,630],{},"llms.txt",", both current, which is the tell of a team that expects machines to read their site.",[51,633,635],{"id":634},"pricing","Pricing",[17,637,638],{},"Prices are in USD, billed monthly, and each tier has a volume dropdown that scales the price.",[640,641,642,664],"table",{},[643,644,645],"thead",{},[646,647,648,652,655,658,661],"tr",{},[649,650,651],"th",{},"Plan",[649,653,654],{},"From",[649,656,657],{},"Images \u002F month",[649,659,660],{},"Batch",[649,662,663],{},"Dedup window",[665,666,667,685,701,717],"tbody",{},[646,668,669,673,676,679,682],{},[670,671,672],"td",{},"Free",[670,674,675],{},"$0",[670,677,678],{},"50",[670,680,681],{},"Not included",[670,683,684],{},"Up to 1 month",[646,686,687,690,693,696,699],{},[670,688,689],{},"Basic",[670,691,692],{},"$14",[670,694,695],{},"1,000 ($14), 3,000 ($29), 10,000 ($69)",[670,697,698],{},"25",[670,700,684],{},[646,702,703,706,709,712,714],{},[670,704,705],{},"Pro",[670,707,708],{},"$149",[670,710,711],{},"30,000 ($149), 65,000 ($249), 100,000 ($375)",[670,713,698],{},[670,715,716],{},"Up to 6 months",[646,718,719,722,725,728,731],{},[670,720,721],{},"Scale",[670,723,724],{},"$749",[670,726,727],{},"200,000 ($749) up to 1,000,000 ($3,000)",[670,729,730],{},"100",[670,732,733],{},"Up to 1 year",[17,735,736],{},"Overages are optional on paid plans at $10 per 1,000 images, or $50 per 10,000 on Scale, so you keep rendering past your limit instead of returning broken images to users. PDFs, dynamic OG images and unlimited image retention start at Basic. S3 storage and proxies unlock from 10,000 images\u002Fmonth. Longer render timeouts, 50 seconds instead of 30, are Scale only. Enterprise is a conversation, with a 99.95% uptime SLA and invoice billing.",[17,738,739,740,742],{},"The free tier deserves a caveat, because it's generous in the dimension people check and narrow in the ones they don't. 50 images a month is plenty to prototype with, and the API, the Template Editor and the MCP server all work on it. But PDFs, batching, OG configs and media uploads in the editor are all gated, and I hit each of those walls before the account was upgraded: batching and proxies refuse with a plain-English reason, and a ",[93,741,536],{}," request comes back as a field-level validation error saying the plan cannot generate PDFs. Clear refusals rather than mysterious failures, but treat the free plan as a trial of the rendering rather than of the product.",[17,744,745],{},"The free tier's template allowance is five, and the API tells you so the moment you ask for a sixth (\"The tryit plan is limited to 5 templates\"). One undocumented rule to know: a template has to contain at least one Handlebars placeholder, so a static layout is rejected until you give it a variable.",[51,747,749],{"id":748},"who-should-use-it","Who should use it",[160,751,752,755,758,761,764],{},[163,753,754],{},"Anyone generating social cards or OG images at volume, especially with a CMS or a blog with hundreds of pages behind it.",[163,756,757],{},"Teams that need URL screenshots inside a product: monitoring, reporting, archiving, thumbnails of customer sites.",[163,759,760],{},"Developers who have already tried running Puppeteer in a Lambda and want the fonts, the timeouts and the memory ceiling to be someone else's problem.",[163,762,763],{},"Anyone building agent workflows that produce visual output, where the MCP server means the assistant never sees a credential.",[163,765,766],{},"Non-developers on a team that already pays for HCTI, who can now maintain a card layout in the editor without opening a pull request.",[17,768,769,770,773],{},"Who shouldn't bother: if you need one image, make it in Figma. If your image generation lives entirely inside a Next.js app, ",[93,771,772],{},"@vercel\u002Fog"," is free and in-process, and HCTI's advantage only shows up once you want templates, screenshots, PDFs and caching from the same place. And on positioning: HCTI sits deliberately between the screenshot-only APIs like Urlbox and ScreenshotOne, which don't do templated graphics, and the design-first templated-image products like Bannerbear and Placid, which are less interested in being a general rendering primitive. HCTI is an API that grew a visual editor, not a design tool that grew an API, and the editor is well enough integrated that the distinction is about priorities rather than polish. If you want brand-managed graphics with a drag-and-drop-first workflow, the design-first tools will feel more natural. If you want one endpoint that renders anything a browser can render, HCTI is built for that job and priced for it.",[51,775,777],{"id":776},"one-last-data-point","One last data point",[17,779,780],{},"I sent this draft to Jeff Needles, who runs HCTI, before publishing. Within a day he had gone through it point by point and shipped fixes for most of what I'd raised. For a service you're about to make a dependency, how quickly the person behind it answers feedback is worth as much as any line in the pricing table.",[51,782,784],{"id":783},"conclusion","Conclusion",[17,786,787,788,790],{},"That's the end of this ",[21,789,25],{"href":23,"rel":24}," review. Eight years in, the core is exactly as boring as it should be: fast, well-documented, predictable, with defaults that are already what you'd have chosen. What's new is more interesting than \"we added an editor\". The Template Editor is a properly thought-out variable system with three explicit missing-value behaviours and a test payload you can see, and the MCP server is one of the better-written ones I've connected to, with a consent screen a non-developer can read and an audit page for every grant.",[17,792,793],{},[37,794,795],{},"What I liked:",[160,797,798,801,804,807,810,813,816,819,822],{},[163,799,800],{},"Deduplication that genuinely cuts the bill, verified: a repeated request returned the same image and cost no credit.",[163,802,803],{},"Sensible defaults, including device scale 2 for HTML and 1 for URLs, so you rarely fight the output size.",[163,805,806],{},"The Template Editor's variable modes, Required, Ignored and Fallback, enforced server-side with a clear 400.",[163,808,809],{},"The rendered template matched the editor canvas exactly.",[163,811,812],{},"29 canvas presets that each cite where the dimensions come from.",[163,814,815],{},"MCP done properly: OAuth with PKCE, plain-English scopes, no key pasted into an assistant, and a sessions page with a Disable button.",[163,817,818],{},"Instructions and error messages written for agents that actually help them self-correct.",[163,820,821],{},"The free Social Card Previewer, which audits your OG tags with character counts and needs no account.",[163,823,824],{},"Sample request code in seven languages that never puts your key on screen.",[17,826,827],{},[37,828,829],{},"Things to keep in mind:",[160,831,832,835,841,850,860],{},[163,833,834],{},"The free plan looks like a full trial but excludes PDFs, batching, OG configs and media uploads.",[163,836,837,838,840],{},"MCP tool arguments nest under a ",[93,839,408],{}," object, which the documented example prompts don't show.",[163,842,843,844,846,847,849],{},"Invented ",[93,845,536],{}," keys are accepted with a ",[93,848,559],{}," and silently ignored, so a wrong page size or margin looks like it worked.",[163,851,852,853,855,856,261,858,335],{},"Editor templates store blocks rather than markup, so ",[93,854,244],{}," returns them with empty ",[93,857,260],{},[93,859,264],{},[163,861,862],{},"It's a rendering primitive, so you still think in boxes and pixels. It won't design the card for you.",[17,864,865,866,868],{},"If you're generating images from a server and you've been putting off the browser problem, ",[21,867,25],{"href":23,"rel":24}," is the well-built way to make it stop being your problem.",{"title":870,"searchDepth":871,"depth":871,"links":872},"",2,[873,874,878,879,880,881,882,883,884,885],{"id":53,"depth":871,"text":54},{"id":121,"depth":871,"text":122,"children":875},[876],{"id":154,"depth":877,"text":155},3,{"id":268,"depth":871,"text":269},{"id":412,"depth":871,"text":413},{"id":466,"depth":871,"text":467},{"id":580,"depth":871,"text":581},{"id":634,"depth":871,"text":635},{"id":748,"depth":871,"text":749},{"id":776,"depth":871,"text":777},{"id":783,"depth":871,"text":784},null,"Reviews","2026-09-15","We've tested HTML\u002FCSS to Image, a rendering API for HTML, live URLs and reusable templates, now with a visual editor and a hosted MCP server.","md",{},true,{"component":894,"props":895},"OgImagePost",{"image":896},"https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Freviews\u002Fhtml-css-to-image\u002Fog_image.jpg","\u002Fblog\u002Fhtml-css-to-image-review",{"title":8,"description":889},{"loc":897},"blog\u002Fhtml-css-to-image-review","vSWoM_CMC4G0FUTH-2CmRNlkks1W_ovsQWN8xcmkChM",{"id":903,"title":904,"author":905,"body":906,"canonical":886,"category":1755,"date":1756,"description":1757,"extension":890,"feedback":886,"meta":1758,"navigation":892,"ogImage":886,"path":1759,"seo":1760,"sitemap":1761,"stem":1762,"thumbnail":1763,"tool_slug":886,"unsponsored":4,"updated":886,"__hash__":1764},"blog\u002Fblog\u002Fsurvey-question-examples.md","100+ Survey Question Examples (and When to Use Each)",{"name":10,"avatar":11,"url":12},{"type":14,"value":907,"toc":1714},[908,911,914,922,926,929,933,936,941,945,948,953,956,960,968,972,975,979,982,986,989,994,998,1001,1046,1049,1053,1065,1068,1119,1122,1128,1132,1135,1195,1198,1202,1205,1245,1252,1256,1259,1297,1301,1304,1337,1340,1344,1347,1383,1387,1390,1429,1433,1436,1496,1500,1503,1549,1552,1556,1560,1563,1567,1570,1574,1577,1581,1584,1588,1591,1595,1598,1602,1605,1609,1612,1616,1619,1623,1626,1630,1633,1647,1653,1665,1669,1673,1676,1680,1683,1687,1690,1694,1697,1701,1707,1711],[17,909,910],{},"A survey is only as good as its questions. Ask something vague and you get vague answers. Ask two things at once and you get answers you cannot use. Ask a leading question and you get the answer you were hoping for, which is worse than no answer at all.",[17,912,913],{},"This page is a reference: more than 100 survey question examples, grouped by what you are trying to learn, with a note on which question type fits and when to send it. Copy them, adapt the wording to your product or audience, and cut anything you will not act on.",[17,915,916,917,921],{},"If you want to turn a list like this into a live survey quickly, ",[21,918,920],{"href":919},"https:\u002F\u002Ffast-surveys.com\u002F?ref=uneed.best&ref_type=adv","Fast Surveys"," has an AI survey generator: describe what you want to learn and it drafts a structured survey you can then edit question by question. It is free up to 50 responses per survey.",[51,923,925],{"id":924},"survey-question-types-briefly","Survey question types, briefly",[17,927,928],{},"The same question can work or fail depending on the format you pick, so here are the six you will use most.",[152,930,932],{"id":931},"multiple-choice","Multiple choice",[17,934,935],{},"The respondent picks one option or several from a list you define. Fast to answer, easy to analyze, and right when you already know the likely answers. Add an \"Other\" option with a text field whenever you are not sure the list is complete.",[17,937,938],{},[69,939,940],{},"Example: \"How did you first hear about us?\" Search engine \u002F Social media \u002F A friend or colleague \u002F Newsletter or blog \u002F Other",[152,942,944],{"id":943},"rating-scale-and-likert-scale","Rating scale and Likert scale",[17,946,947],{},"A rating scale asks for a score (1 to 5 stars, 1 to 10). A Likert scale asks how much someone agrees with a statement, usually on five points from \"Strongly disagree\" to \"Strongly agree\". Both measure intensity and track change over time well, as long as the scale stays identical between surveys. Label at least both ends, so a \"3\" means the same thing to everyone.",[17,949,950],{},[69,951,952],{},"Example: \"The setup instructions were easy to follow.\" Strongly disagree \u002F Disagree \u002F Neutral \u002F Agree \u002F Strongly agree",[17,954,955],{},"Mood scales (a row of faces or emoji) are a lighter variant: less precise, but quick and friendly for short surveys shown in context.",[152,957,959],{"id":958},"net-promoter-score-nps","Net Promoter Score (NPS)",[17,961,962,963,967],{},"One specific question, \"How likely are you to recommend ",[964,965,966],"span",{},"product"," to a friend or colleague?\", answered on a 0 to 10 scale. It gets its own section below, because it is scored differently from a normal rating.",[152,969,971],{"id":970},"open-ended","Open-ended",[17,973,974],{},"A text box. Open questions give you the words your customers actually use, the reasons behind a score, and the things you did not think to ask about. The cost is effort: they are slower to answer and slower to analyze. Use one or two per survey, ideally right after a closed question (\"What is the main reason for your score?\").",[152,976,978],{"id":977},"yesno","Yes\u002Fno",[17,980,981],{},"Binary questions are the easiest to answer and to count. Use them for facts (\"Did you contact support in the last 30 days?\") and to route people to the right follow-up questions. Avoid them for opinions, where the honest answer is rarely a clean yes or no.",[152,983,985],{"id":984},"ranking","Ranking",[17,987,988],{},"The respondent orders a list from most to least important. Ranking forces trade-offs, which a rating scale does not: given the chance, people rate every feature \"very important\". Keep the list to five to seven items, or the middle turns into noise.",[17,990,991],{},[69,992,993],{},"Example: \"Rank these in order of importance to you: price, speed, integrations, customer support, ease of use.\"",[51,995,997],{"id":996},"customer-satisfaction-survey-questions","Customer satisfaction survey questions",[17,999,1000],{},"Customer satisfaction (CSAT) questions measure how people feel about a specific interaction or about your product as a whole. Keep the survey short and send it close to the moment you are asking about.",[1002,1003,1004,1010,1013,1019,1025,1028,1031,1034,1037,1040,1043],"ol",{},[163,1005,1006,1007,1009],{},"Overall, how satisfied are you with ",[964,1008,966],{},"? (1 to 5, Very dissatisfied to Very satisfied)",[163,1011,1012],{},"How satisfied were you with your most recent purchase?",[163,1014,1015,1016,1018],{},"How well does ",[964,1017,966],{}," meet your needs? (Not at all \u002F Slightly \u002F Moderately \u002F Very well \u002F Extremely well)",[163,1020,1021,1022,1024],{},"How would you rate the quality of ",[964,1023,966],{}," relative to its price?",[163,1026,1027],{},"How easy was it to get what you needed today? (Very difficult to Very easy)",[163,1029,1030],{},"How satisfied were you with the speed of our response?",[163,1032,1033],{},"Did our support team resolve your issue? (Yes \u002F Partly \u002F No)",[163,1035,1036],{},"How would you rate the knowledge of the person who helped you?",[163,1038,1039],{},"What did you like most about your experience with us?",[163,1041,1042],{},"Is there anything that almost stopped you from buying?",[163,1044,1045],{},"What is one thing we could do to improve your experience?",[17,1047,1048],{},"Question 5 is a customer effort question. It is often more useful than satisfaction itself, because \"this was hard\" points straight at something you can fix.",[51,1050,1052],{"id":1051},"nps-and-customer-loyalty-survey-questions","NPS and customer loyalty survey questions",[17,1054,1055,1056,1060,1061,335],{},"NPS is a standard question with a standard scoring method, which is what makes it useful for comparing yourself over time. As ",[21,1057,1059],{"href":1058},"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.netpromotersystem.com\u002Fabout\u002Fmeasuring-your-net-promoter-score\u002F","Bain & Company defines it",", people who answer 9 or 10 are promoters, 7 or 8 are passives, and 0 to 6 are detractors. Your score is the percentage of promoters minus the percentage of detractors, so it lands anywhere between -100 and +100. Once your responses are in, ",[21,1062,1064],{"href":1063},"\u002Ffree-tools\u002Fnps-calculator","calculate your score with our free NPS calculator",[17,1066,1067],{},"The score on its own tells you very little. The follow-up question is where the value is.",[1002,1069,1071,1077,1080,1083,1089,1095,1098,1104,1110,1113],{"start":1070},12,[163,1072,1073,1074,1076],{},"How likely are you to recommend ",[964,1075,966],{}," to a friend or colleague? (0 to 10)",[163,1078,1079],{},"What is the main reason for your score?",[163,1081,1082],{},"What would it take for you to give us a 9 or 10? (for passives and detractors)",[163,1084,1085,1086,1088],{},"What do you value most about ",[964,1087,966],{},"? (for promoters)",[163,1090,1091,1092,1094],{},"Have you recommended ",[964,1093,966],{}," to anyone in the last three months? (Yes \u002F No)",[163,1096,1097],{},"How likely are you to buy from us again? (Very unlikely to Very likely)",[163,1099,1100,1101,1103],{},"How would you feel if you could no longer use ",[964,1102,966],{},"? (Very disappointed \u002F Somewhat disappointed \u002F Not disappointed)",[163,1105,1106,1107,1109],{},"What would you use instead if ",[964,1108,966],{}," disappeared?",[163,1111,1112],{},"How likely are you to renew your subscription when it expires?",[163,1114,1115,1116,1118],{},"Compared to other products you have tried, is ",[964,1117,966],{}," better, about the same, or worse?",[17,1120,1121],{},"Question 18 is a common product\u002Fmarket fit check: the share of people who would be \"very disappointed\" shows how much your users actually rely on you, which a satisfaction score does not.",[17,1123,1124,1125,1127],{},"If you want NPS answers from people while they are on your site, rather than from whoever opens an email a week later, ",[21,1126,920],{"href":919}," offers NPS as one of its website feedback widget formats, and the answers land in the same dashboard as your surveys.",[51,1129,1131],{"id":1130},"product-feedback-survey-questions","Product feedback survey questions",[17,1133,1134],{},"These go to existing users. The goal is to learn what to build, fix or remove, so ask about behavior and problems rather than hypothetical features.",[1002,1136,1138,1144,1147,1150,1156,1162,1168,1174,1177,1183,1189],{"start":1137},22,[163,1139,1140,1141,1143],{},"What do you mainly use ",[964,1142,966],{}," for?",[163,1145,1146],{},"Which feature do you use most often?",[163,1148,1149],{},"Which feature do you use least, or never? Why?",[163,1151,1152,1153,1155],{},"What is the one thing ",[964,1154,966],{}," does not do that you wish it did?",[163,1157,1015,1158,1161],{},[964,1159,1160],{},"feature"," work for you? (1 to 5)",[163,1163,1164,1165,1167],{},"When did you last feel frustrated using ",[964,1166,966],{},"? What happened?",[163,1169,1170,1171,1173],{},"What were you using before ",[964,1172,966],{},"?",[163,1175,1176],{},"Rank these planned improvements in order of importance to you. (ranking)",[163,1178,1179,1180,1182],{},"How often do you use ",[964,1181,966],{},"? (Daily \u002F Weekly \u002F Monthly \u002F Less often)",[163,1184,1185,1186,1188],{},"If you could change one thing about ",[964,1187,966],{},", what would it be?",[163,1190,1191,1192,1194],{},"Is there anything in ",[964,1193,966],{}," that feels confusing or hard to find?",[17,1196,1197],{},"Be careful with \"Would you use a feature that does X?\". Almost everyone says yes to a free hypothetical. Ask about the problem instead: \"How do you handle X today?\" What people already do is a much better signal than what they say they might do.",[51,1199,1201],{"id":1200},"website-and-ux-feedback-survey-questions","Website and UX feedback survey questions",[17,1203,1204],{},"Website questions work best in context. A small prompt on the page, shown at the right moment, beats an email a week later when nobody remembers what the page looked like. Stick to one or two questions per prompt.",[1002,1206,1208,1211,1214,1221,1224,1227,1230,1233,1236,1239,1242],{"start":1207},33,[163,1209,1210],{},"Did you find what you were looking for today? (Yes \u002F Partly \u002F No)",[163,1212,1213],{},"What were you trying to do on this page?",[163,1215,1216,1217,1220],{},"How easy was it to find ",[964,1218,1219],{},"information or product","? (Very difficult to Very easy)",[163,1222,1223],{},"What, if anything, stopped you from completing your purchase today?",[163,1225,1226],{},"Was this article helpful? (Yes \u002F No)",[163,1228,1229],{},"What is missing from this page?",[163,1231,1232],{},"How would you rate the look and feel of our website? (1 to 5)",[163,1234,1235],{},"Did anything on this page not work as expected?",[163,1237,1238],{},"How did you arrive at our website today?",[163,1240,1241],{},"What almost stopped you from signing up?",[163,1243,1244],{},"Is the pricing on this page clear? (Yes \u002F No, with \"What is unclear?\" as a follow-up)",[17,1246,1247,1248,1251],{},"This is exactly what ",[21,1249,1250],{"href":919},"Fast Surveys' website feedback widgets"," are for. You paste a small snippet into your site, pick a format (quick choices, mood ratings or written feedback), and every response goes to the same place as your regular surveys, so \"Was this article helpful?\" on your docs and your quarterly customer survey sit side by side.",[51,1253,1255],{"id":1254},"onboarding-survey-questions","Onboarding survey questions",[17,1257,1258],{},"Onboarding questions do two jobs: they help you tailor the first experience, and they show you where new users get stuck. Ask the profile questions at sign-up (and keep them short), and the experience questions after a few days of use.",[1002,1260,1262,1265,1270,1273,1276,1279,1282,1285,1291,1294],{"start":1261},44,[163,1263,1264],{},"What is your role? (multiple choice)",[163,1266,1267,1268,1173],{},"What is the main thing you want to achieve with ",[964,1269,966],{},[163,1271,1272],{},"How big is your team? (Just me \u002F 2-10 \u002F 11-50 \u002F 51-200 \u002F More than 200)",[163,1274,1275],{},"How did you hear about us?",[163,1277,1278],{},"Have you used a similar tool before? Which one?",[163,1280,1281],{},"How easy was it to get started? (Very difficult to Very easy)",[163,1283,1284],{},"Was there any step in the setup that confused you?",[163,1286,1287,1288,1290],{},"How long did it take before ",[964,1289,966],{}," was useful to you? (Minutes \u002F Hours \u002F Days \u002F Not yet)",[163,1292,1293],{},"What nearly made you give up during setup?",[163,1295,1296],{},"Is there anything you expected to find that was not there?",[51,1298,1300],{"id":1299},"churn-and-cancellation-survey-questions","Churn and cancellation survey questions",[17,1302,1303],{},"A cancellation survey is the last conversation you will have with a customer, so make it short and respectful. One required multiple choice question and one optional text field are enough. Never make people fill in a form before they are allowed to leave.",[1002,1305,1307,1310,1313,1316,1319,1325,1328,1331,1334],{"start":1306},54,[163,1308,1309],{},"What is the main reason you are canceling? (Too expensive \u002F Missing features \u002F Switching to another product \u002F No longer need it \u002F Technical issues \u002F Other)",[163,1311,1312],{},"What could we have done to keep you?",[163,1314,1315],{},"Which product are you switching to?",[163,1317,1318],{},"Was there a specific moment when you decided to cancel?",[163,1320,1321,1322,1324],{},"Did ",[964,1323,966],{}," do what you expected when you signed up? (Yes \u002F Partly \u002F No)",[163,1326,1327],{},"How would you rate your overall experience with us? (1 to 5)",[163,1329,1330],{},"Would you consider coming back in the future? (Yes \u002F Maybe \u002F No)",[163,1332,1333],{},"If we fixed the issue you mentioned, would you stay? (Yes \u002F No)",[163,1335,1336],{},"Is there anything else you would like us to know?",[17,1338,1339],{},"Question 56 tends to be the most revealing. If a lot of people leave for the same competitor, that tells you more than any satisfaction score.",[51,1341,1343],{"id":1342},"event-feedback-survey-questions","Event feedback survey questions",[17,1345,1346],{},"Send it within a day, while people still remember the details, and split your questions between content, logistics and the event overall. For an in-person event, a QR code on the last slide or on the tables lets people answer before they leave instead of digging through their inbox later.",[1002,1348,1350,1353,1356,1359,1362,1365,1368,1371,1374,1377,1380],{"start":1349},63,[163,1351,1352],{},"Overall, how would you rate the event? (1 to 5)",[163,1354,1355],{},"How likely are you to attend our next event? (Very unlikely to Very likely)",[163,1357,1358],{},"Which session was the most valuable to you?",[163,1360,1361],{},"Which session was the least valuable?",[163,1363,1364],{},"How relevant was the content to your work? (Not at all to Extremely relevant)",[163,1366,1367],{},"How would you rate the speakers?",[163,1369,1370],{},"Was the event the right length? (Too short \u002F About right \u002F Too long)",[163,1372,1373],{},"How would you rate the venue (or the streaming platform, for an online event)?",[163,1375,1376],{},"What was the main reason you attended?",[163,1378,1379],{},"What topics would you like us to cover next time?",[163,1381,1382],{},"What is one thing we should change for next time?",[51,1384,1386],{"id":1385},"employee-engagement-survey-questions","Employee engagement survey questions",[17,1388,1389],{},"Employee surveys only work if people trust that their answers are anonymous and that something will happen afterwards. Say who will see the results, then share what you learned and what you are going to change. Most of these work best as Likert statements.",[1002,1391,1393,1396,1399,1402,1405,1408,1411,1414,1420,1423,1426],{"start":1392},74,[163,1394,1395],{},"How satisfied are you with your job overall? (1 to 5)",[163,1397,1398],{},"I understand how my work contributes to the company's goals. (Likert)",[163,1400,1401],{},"I have the tools and resources I need to do my job well. (Likert)",[163,1403,1404],{},"My manager gives me useful feedback. (Likert)",[163,1406,1407],{},"I feel my work is recognized. (Likert)",[163,1409,1410],{},"How manageable is your current workload? (Far too light to Far too heavy)",[163,1412,1413],{},"I can see a path to grow my career here. (Likert)",[163,1415,1073,1416,1419],{},[964,1417,1418],{},"company"," as a place to work? (0 to 10, often called eNPS)",[163,1421,1422],{},"Do you see yourself working here a year from now? (Yes \u002F Not sure \u002F No)",[163,1424,1425],{},"What is one thing that would make your work easier?",[163,1427,1428],{},"What should leadership start, stop or keep doing?",[51,1430,1432],{"id":1431},"market-research-survey-questions","Market research survey questions",[17,1434,1435],{},"Market research surveys usually go to people who are not your customers yet. Screen first, so you know who answered, then ask about the problem and their current behavior before you ever mention your product.",[1002,1437,1439,1442,1448,1455,1462,1465,1471,1477,1483,1486,1493],{"start":1438},85,[163,1440,1441],{},"Which of these best describes you? (screening question, multiple choice)",[163,1443,1444,1445,1173],{},"How do you currently handle ",[964,1446,1447],{},"problem",[163,1449,1450,1451,1454],{},"What tools or services do you use for ",[964,1452,1453],{},"task"," today?",[163,1456,1457,1458,1461],{},"Roughly how much do you spend on ",[964,1459,1460],{},"category"," per month?",[163,1463,1464],{},"What frustrates you most about your current solution?",[163,1466,1467,1468,1470],{},"How important is solving ",[964,1469,1447],{}," for you right now? (Not a priority to Top priority)",[163,1472,1473,1474,1476],{},"When did you last look for a new ",[964,1475,1460],{}," product?",[163,1478,1479,1480,1482],{},"Rank these factors by how much they matter when you choose a ",[964,1481,1460],{}," product. (ranking)",[163,1484,1485],{},"Where do you usually find out about new tools? (multiple choice)",[163,1487,1488,1489,1492],{},"At what price would ",[964,1490,1491],{},"product description"," feel too expensive to consider?",[163,1494,1495],{},"What would make you switch from what you use today?",[51,1497,1499],{"id":1498},"content-creator-and-audience-survey-questions","Content creator and audience survey questions",[17,1501,1502],{},"If you run a newsletter, a YouTube channel or a podcast, an audience survey tells you what to make next and which sponsors your audience will actually welcome. Keep it under five minutes, and say what you will do with the answers.",[1002,1504,1506,1512,1515,1518,1521,1524,1527,1530,1533,1536,1543],{"start":1505},96,[163,1507,1508,1509,1173],{},"How did you find my ",[964,1510,1511],{},"newsletter, channel or podcast",[163,1513,1514],{},"How long have you been following? (Less than a month \u002F 1 to 6 months \u002F 6 to 12 months \u002F More than a year)",[163,1516,1517],{},"Which type of content do you enjoy most? (multiple choice)",[163,1519,1520],{},"What topic would you like me to cover next?",[163,1522,1523],{},"How often would you like to hear from me? (More often \u002F Same as now \u002F Less often)",[163,1525,1526],{},"What do you do for a living? (useful for sponsor fit)",[163,1528,1529],{},"Have you ever bought something because I recommended it? (Yes \u002F No)",[163,1531,1532],{},"Which of these products would you be interested in hearing about? (check a sponsor before you commit to promoting it)",[163,1534,1535],{},"Would you pay for extra content, like a paid tier or a course? (Yes \u002F Maybe \u002F No)",[163,1537,1538,1539,1542],{},"Which recent ",[964,1540,1541],{},"issue, video or episode"," did you like most?",[163,1544,1545,1546,1173],{},"What is the one thing you would change about the ",[964,1547,1548],{},"newsletter or channel",[17,1550,1551],{},"If a full survey feels like too much, ask one of these per issue. You will build the same picture over a few weeks.",[51,1553,1555],{"id":1554},"mistakes-to-avoid","Mistakes to avoid",[152,1557,1559],{"id":1558},"leading-questions","Leading questions",[17,1561,1562],{},"A leading question suggests the answer. \"How much did you enjoy our new dashboard?\" assumes the person enjoyed it. Ask \"How would you rate the new dashboard?\" instead.",[152,1564,1566],{"id":1565},"loaded-questions","Loaded questions",[17,1568,1569],{},"A loaded question contains an assumption that may not be true. \"What do you like most about our fast delivery?\" is useless to someone whose order arrived late. Check the assumption first with a yes\u002Fno question, then ask the follow-up only to the people it applies to.",[152,1571,1573],{"id":1572},"double-barreled-questions","Double-barreled questions",[17,1575,1576],{},"\"How satisfied are you with our pricing and customer support?\" is two questions. Someone who loves your support and hates your pricing has no honest answer. Split it in two.",[152,1578,1580],{"id":1579},"absolute-words","Absolute words",[17,1582,1583],{},"\"Always\", \"never\", \"every\" and \"all\" push people towards no. \"Do you always read our newsletter?\" gets a no from someone who reads nine issues out of ten. Ask about frequency instead.",[152,1585,1587],{"id":1586},"unbalanced-scales","Unbalanced scales",[17,1589,1590],{},"\"Poor \u002F Good \u002F Very good \u002F Excellent\" has three positive options and one negative, so it inflates your results. Give the scale as many points on the negative side as on the positive side.",[152,1592,1594],{"id":1593},"overlapping-or-incomplete-options","Overlapping or incomplete options",[17,1596,1597],{},"\"0-10 employees\" and \"10-50 employees\" overlap. A list without \"Other\" or \"Not applicable\" forces people to pick something false. Both give you data you cannot trust.",[152,1599,1601],{"id":1600},"jargon-and-internal-names","Jargon and internal names",[17,1603,1604],{},"Your team knows what \"the Insights tab\" or \"SSO\" means. Many of your respondents may not, and they will answer anyway. Use the words your customers use.",[152,1606,1608],{"id":1607},"asking-about-the-future-instead-of-the-past","Asking about the future instead of the past",[17,1610,1611],{},"\"Would you pay $10 a month for this?\" measures politeness. \"What did you last pay for something like this?\" measures behavior.",[152,1613,1615],{"id":1614},"too-many-questions","Too many questions",[17,1617,1618],{},"Every question should map to a decision you will make. If you would not act on the answer, cut it. Put your most important question first in case people drop off, say how long the survey takes, and make sure that is true.",[152,1620,1622],{"id":1621},"required-open-ended-questions","Required open-ended questions",[17,1624,1625],{},"Forcing someone to type an answer mostly gets you \"n\u002Fa\" and \"asdf\". Keep text fields optional, and they will be filled by the people who have something to say.",[51,1627,1629],{"id":1628},"build-your-survey","Build your survey",[17,1631,1632],{},"Once you have your questions, the process is short:",[1002,1634,1635,1638,1641,1644],{},[163,1636,1637],{},"Write down the decision the survey should inform, and keep only the questions that feed it.",[163,1639,1640],{},"Put quick, closed questions first and open ones towards the end.",[163,1642,1643],{},"Test it on two or three people before sending. You will always find one question they read differently from how you meant it.",[163,1645,1646],{},"Send it where people already are: in the app, on the page, by email, or as a QR code at an event.",[17,1648,1649,1650,1652],{},"For the building part, ",[21,1651,920],{"href":919}," is a survey builder made in the UK that covers most of this page. It has multiple choice, checkboxes, dropdowns, mood scales, text fields and image questions, an AI generator that drafts a survey from a short description, and AI summaries of the written answers once they come in. You can share by link or QR code, embed feedback widgets on your website, match your brand colours, and invite unlimited team members. The free plan includes unlimited surveys with up to 50 responses per survey and a limited set of question types. The Pro plan ($29 a month at the time of writing) unlocks all question types and unlimited responses. Fast Surveys is a Uneed partner.",[17,1654,1655,1656,1660,1661,335],{},"Want to compare options first? Browse ",[21,1657,1659],{"href":1658},"\u002Fbest-products-by-tag\u002Fsurveys","the survey tools listed on Uneed",", or, if you are moving away from Typeform, see our ",[21,1662,1664],{"href":1663},"\u002Falternatives\u002Ftypeform","Typeform alternatives",[51,1666,1668],{"id":1667},"faq","FAQ",[152,1670,1672],{"id":1671},"what-makes-a-good-survey-question","What makes a good survey question?",[17,1674,1675],{},"A good survey question is specific, neutral, and asks about one thing at a time. The respondent can answer it from their own experience, and the answer helps you make a decision. If a question fails any of those, rewrite it or cut it.",[152,1677,1679],{"id":1678},"how-many-questions-should-a-survey-have","How many questions should a survey have?",[17,1681,1682],{},"As few as you need to make your decision. A widget on a web page should ask one or two. A post-purchase or post-support survey works well with one to three. A yearly customer or employee survey can be longer, as long as you tell people upfront how long it will take.",[152,1684,1686],{"id":1685},"what-is-the-difference-between-open-ended-and-closed-ended-questions","What is the difference between open-ended and closed-ended questions?",[17,1688,1689],{},"Closed-ended questions (multiple choice, rating, yes\u002Fno, ranking) offer fixed options, so answers are easy to count and compare. Open-ended questions let people answer in their own words: slower to analyze, but they tell you why. Most good surveys are mostly closed, with one or two open questions.",[152,1691,1693],{"id":1692},"what-is-a-likert-scale-question","What is a Likert scale question?",[17,1695,1696],{},"A Likert scale question presents a statement and asks how much the respondent agrees with it, usually on five points: Strongly disagree, Disagree, Neutral, Agree, Strongly agree. It is the standard format for employee engagement and attitude surveys.",[152,1698,1700],{"id":1699},"how-do-you-calculate-nps","How do you calculate NPS?",[17,1702,1703,1704,335],{},"Take the percentage of respondents who answered 9 or 10 (promoters) and subtract the percentage who answered 0 to 6 (detractors). Passives (7 or 8) count in the total but in neither group. For example, if 200 people answer, 120 are promoters (60%) and 30 are detractors (15%), your NPS is 60 minus 15, so 45. Or skip the arithmetic with our ",[21,1705,1706],{"href":1063},"free NPS calculator",[152,1708,1710],{"id":1709},"how-do-i-get-more-people-to-answer-my-survey","How do I get more people to answer my survey?",[17,1712,1713],{},"Keep it short and say how long it takes. Ask close to the experience you are asking about, and reach people where they already are. Then show that you acted on the answers: people who see their feedback lead to a change are more willing to answer next time.",{"title":870,"searchDepth":871,"depth":871,"links":1715},[1716,1724,1725,1726,1727,1728,1729,1730,1731,1732,1733,1734,1746,1747],{"id":924,"depth":871,"text":925,"children":1717},[1718,1719,1720,1721,1722,1723],{"id":931,"depth":877,"text":932},{"id":943,"depth":877,"text":944},{"id":958,"depth":877,"text":959},{"id":970,"depth":877,"text":971},{"id":977,"depth":877,"text":978},{"id":984,"depth":877,"text":985},{"id":996,"depth":871,"text":997},{"id":1051,"depth":871,"text":1052},{"id":1130,"depth":871,"text":1131},{"id":1200,"depth":871,"text":1201},{"id":1254,"depth":871,"text":1255},{"id":1299,"depth":871,"text":1300},{"id":1342,"depth":871,"text":1343},{"id":1385,"depth":871,"text":1386},{"id":1431,"depth":871,"text":1432},{"id":1498,"depth":871,"text":1499},{"id":1554,"depth":871,"text":1555,"children":1735},[1736,1737,1738,1739,1740,1741,1742,1743,1744,1745],{"id":1558,"depth":877,"text":1559},{"id":1565,"depth":877,"text":1566},{"id":1572,"depth":877,"text":1573},{"id":1579,"depth":877,"text":1580},{"id":1586,"depth":877,"text":1587},{"id":1593,"depth":877,"text":1594},{"id":1600,"depth":877,"text":1601},{"id":1607,"depth":877,"text":1608},{"id":1614,"depth":877,"text":1615},{"id":1621,"depth":877,"text":1622},{"id":1628,"depth":871,"text":1629},{"id":1667,"depth":871,"text":1668,"children":1748},[1749,1750,1751,1752,1753,1754],{"id":1671,"depth":877,"text":1672},{"id":1678,"depth":877,"text":1679},{"id":1685,"depth":877,"text":1686},{"id":1692,"depth":877,"text":1693},{"id":1699,"depth":877,"text":1700},{"id":1709,"depth":877,"text":1710},"Marketing","2026-09-10","100+ survey question examples for customer satisfaction, NPS, product feedback, churn, events, employees and market research, plus the mistakes to avoid.",{},"\u002Fblog\u002Fsurvey-question-examples",{"title":904,"description":1757},{"loc":1759},"blog\u002Fsurvey-question-examples","\u002Fblog\u002Fsurvey-question-examples.jpeg","-OBaWN6GCZI1ZnsWxo7VbFQXeiapDIz-7ksDyIxoc1Y",{"id":1766,"title":1767,"author":1768,"body":1769,"canonical":886,"category":2409,"date":2410,"description":2411,"extension":890,"feedback":886,"meta":2412,"navigation":892,"ogImage":886,"path":2413,"seo":2414,"sitemap":2415,"stem":2416,"thumbnail":2417,"tool_slug":886,"unsponsored":4,"updated":886,"__hash__":2418},"blog\u002Fblog\u002Fbest-ai-tools-for-churches.md","The Best AI Tools for Churches and Pastors in 2026",{"name":10,"avatar":11,"url":12},{"type":14,"value":1770,"toc":2382},[1771,1774,1777,1780,1784,1788,1802,1805,1813,1819,1825,1829,1835,1840,1844,1848,1854,1859,1864,1868,1872,1878,1883,1887,1891,1897,1902,1906,1910,1916,1921,1925,1929,1933,1939,1944,1948,1952,1958,1963,1967,1971,1977,1982,1987,1991,1994,1998,2004,2009,2013,2017,2023,2028,2032,2036,2046,2051,2055,2059,2065,2070,2074,2078,2277,2281,2346,2350,2357,2360,2362,2374],[17,1772,1773],{},"Most pastors have already tried asking ChatGPT for help with a sermon. It works, until it quotes a translation you do not preach from, treats every doctrine as equally valid, or hands back filler that could belong to any church of any tradition.",[17,1775,1776],{},"That gap is why a whole category of church-specific AI has appeared over the last two years. Some of it is genuinely useful. A lot of it is a thin wrapper around a general model with a cross in the logo.",[17,1778,1779],{},"We list and test tools on Uneed all year, so here are the ones actually worth your Tuesday morning, organized by the job you are trying to get done 🙏",[51,1781,1783],{"id":1782},"best-ai-for-sermon-prep-and-deep-study","Best AI for Sermon Prep and Deep Study",[152,1785,1787],{"id":1786},"alignedai","AlignedAI",[17,1789,1790,1793,1794,1797,1798,1801],{},[21,1791,1787],{"href":1792},"\u002Ftool\u002Falignedai"," is the most complete of the bunch and, unusually for this depth, it is free. Before you type anything it asks for your ",[37,1795,1796],{},"denomination"," (fifteen options) and your ",[37,1799,1800],{},"Bible translation"," (ESV, NIV, KJV, NASB), then shapes every answer, sermon and study around those two choices.",[17,1803,1804],{},"The deep study tool is the standout. Ask about a Greek word and you get the script, the transliteration, the Strong's number as a clickable reference, and the root verb, laid out like a seminary handout rather than a chatbot reply. On top of that sit an AI sermon writer, sermon repurposing into devotions and Bible studies, a personal assistant reachable by SMS, and automated flows across 40+ integrations.",[17,1806,1807,1808,1812],{},"We put it through a full hands-on test: read the ",[21,1809,1811],{"href":1810},"\u002Fblog\u002Faligned-ai-review","AlignedAI review"," for the detail.",[17,1814,1815,1818],{},[37,1816,1817],{},"Best for",": Sermon prep, exegesis with Strong's, doctrinally consistent answers",[17,1820,1821,1824],{},[37,1822,1823],{},"Price",": Free",[152,1826,1828],{"id":1827},"ipreachai","iPreach.ai",[17,1830,1831,1834],{},[21,1832,1828],{"href":1833},"\u002Ftool\u002Fipreach.ai"," is narrower and gets to a draft faster. It was built after the founder watched his sister struggle with sermon prep at her church, and it shows in the scope: you give it an audience and a direction, it produces a personalized sermon you can edit. If AlignedAI feels like more tool than you need, this is the lighter option.",[17,1836,1837,1839],{},[37,1838,1817],{},": Getting a sermon draft quickly, Christian educators",[17,1841,1842,1824],{},[37,1843,1823],{},[152,1845,1847],{"id":1846},"versemap-ai","VerseMap AI",[17,1849,1850,1853],{},[21,1851,1847],{"href":1852},"\u002Ftool\u002Fversemap-ai"," sits between a study Bible and a notebook. Enter any verse and it returns context, theology, original language notes and related passages in one place. The differentiator is VerseChat, which remembers your past notes and questions and folds them back into new commentary and cross references. Journals, sermon summaries and memory games round it out.",[17,1855,1856,1858],{},[37,1857,1817],{},": Verse-level study that builds on itself over months",[17,1860,1861,1863],{},[37,1862,1823],{},": Freemium",[51,1865,1867],{"id":1866},"best-free-ai-bible-study-companions","Best Free AI Bible Study Companions",[152,1869,1871],{"id":1870},"bible-daily","Bible Daily",[17,1873,1874,1877],{},[21,1875,1871],{"href":1876},"\u002Ftool\u002Fbible-daily"," is an AI chat built around scripture: ask a question about love, faith or a modern dilemma and it links you back to the passages that speak to it. Multilingual, which matters more than people expect in mixed congregations.",[17,1879,1880,1882],{},[37,1881,1817],{},": Daily questions, non-English speakers",[17,1884,1885,1824],{},[37,1886,1823],{},[152,1888,1890],{"id":1889},"israelitebot","IsraeliteBot",[17,1892,1893,1896],{},[21,1894,1890],{"href":1895},"\u002Ftool\u002Fisraelitebot:-your-bible-ai-companion-"," answers biblical questions through the lens of Israelite tradition and history. Narrow by design, and useful precisely because of it if that is the angle you study from.",[17,1898,1899,1901],{},[37,1900,1817],{},": Israelite history and tradition",[17,1903,1904,1863],{},[37,1905,1823],{},[152,1907,1909],{"id":1908},"sanctuary-faith","Sanctuary Faith",[17,1911,1912,1915],{},[21,1913,1909],{"href":1914},"\u002Ftool\u002Fsanctuary-faith"," is aimed at people at the edges rather than at the pulpit: questioning, deconstructing, or simply not sure where they stand. It combines a conversational AI with a community, worship music and prayer tools, and it is explicit about being a no-judgment space. Worth knowing about if people in your church are quietly in that place.",[17,1917,1918,1920],{},[37,1919,1817],{},": Faith questions people are not ready to ask out loud",[17,1922,1923,1824],{},[37,1924,1823],{},[51,1926,1928],{"id":1927},"ai-for-prayer-and-personal-devotion","AI for Prayer and Personal Devotion",[152,1930,1932],{"id":1931},"prayer-generator","Prayer Generator",[17,1934,1935,1938],{},[21,1936,1932],{"href":1937},"\u002Ftool\u002Fprayer-generator"," does one thing: writes a personalized prayer for a specific occasion. Useful for hospital visits, weddings and funerals when you need words that fit the person rather than the template.",[17,1940,1941,1943],{},[37,1942,1817],{},": Occasion-specific prayers",[17,1945,1946,1824],{},[37,1947,1823],{},[152,1949,1951],{"id":1950},"dreamnl","Dreamnl",[17,1953,1954,1957],{},[21,1955,1951],{"href":1956},"\u002Ftool\u002Fdreamnl"," is a Christian dream journal, and it is careful about where it stops. It does not interpret, citing Genesis 40:8 as the reason. What it does is summarize the dream, ask you reflection questions, and offer a prayer to bring it before God. A rare example of an AI product with a deliberate theological boundary built into the product itself.",[17,1959,1960,1962],{},[37,1961,1817],{},": Reflective journaling without decoding",[17,1964,1965,1824],{},[37,1966,1823],{},[152,1968,1970],{"id":1969},"bible-mood","Bible Mood",[17,1972,1973,1976],{},[21,1974,1970],{"href":1975},"\u002Ftool\u002Fbible-mood"," starts from a feeling rather than a verse. Pick or describe an emotion and it returns tailored passages, then devotional readings, guided prayers and reflection questions on top. The closest thing here to pastoral care in an app.",[17,1978,1979,1981],{},[37,1980,1817],{},": Mental wellbeing grounded in scripture",[17,1983,1984,1986],{},[37,1985,1823],{},": Paid",[51,1988,1990],{"id":1989},"running-the-church-not-just-the-sermon","Running the Church, Not Just the Sermon",[17,1992,1993],{},"Sermon prep is the loud part of the week. These cover the rest of it.",[152,1995,1997],{"id":1996},"chmeetings","ChMeetings",[17,1999,2000,2003],{},[21,2001,1997],{"href":2002},"\u002Ftool\u002Fchmeetings"," is full church management: membership, giving, accounting, events, groups. It scales from a single small congregation to a multi-campus ministry, and there is a free tier to start on.",[17,2005,2006,2008],{},[37,2007,1817],{},": Church administration in one place",[17,2010,2011,1863],{},[37,2012,1823],{},[152,2014,2016],{"id":2015},"flockhaven","FlockHaven",[17,2018,2019,2022],{},[21,2020,2016],{"href":2021},"\u002Ftool\u002Fflockhaven"," is a website builder made only for small and medium churches. No code, no feature bloat, and none of the generic template wrestling that comes with a general builder.",[17,2024,2025,2027],{},[37,2026,1817],{},": Getting a church website live fast",[17,2029,2030,1986],{},[37,2031,1823],{},[152,2033,2035],{"id":2034},"playlistchurch","playlist.church",[17,2037,2038,2041,2042,2045],{},[21,2039,2035],{"href":2040},"\u002Ftool\u002Fplaylist-church"," ranks worship songs by what churches ",[37,2043,2044],{},"actually sing",", not by sales or editorial picks: 34,889 songs pulled from 1,002 public church playlists across 34 countries. Search by theme, Scripture or season, plan a set, then open it in Spotify, YouTube or Apple Music.",[17,2047,2048,2050],{},[37,2049,1817],{},": Worship leaders planning a set",[17,2052,2053,1824],{},[37,2054,1823],{},[152,2056,2058],{"id":2057},"gospelchannel","GospelChannel",[17,2060,2061,2064],{},[21,2062,2058],{"href":2063},"\u002Ftool\u002Fgospelchannel"," solves the newcomer problem from the other side. Over 3,000 churches in 71 countries, each page carrying the actual worship playlist and sermon videos, so a visitor knows what they are walking into before Sunday. Worth adding your church to.",[17,2066,2067,2069],{},[37,2068,1817],{},": Being found by people new to your area",[17,2071,2072,1824],{},[37,2073,1823],{},[51,2075,2077],{"id":2076},"comparison-table","Comparison Table",[640,2079,2080,2098],{},[643,2081,2082],{},[646,2083,2084,2087,2090,2093,2096],{},[649,2085,2086],{},"Tool",[649,2088,2089],{},"Job",[649,2091,2092],{},"Free tier",[649,2094,2095],{},"Denomination-aware",[649,2097,1823],{},[665,2099,2100,2115,2130,2145,2158,2171,2184,2197,2210,2225,2238,2251,2264],{},[646,2101,2102,2104,2107,2110,2113],{},[670,2103,1787],{},[670,2105,2106],{},"Sermon prep, deep study, assistant",[670,2108,2109],{},"✅ Fully free",[670,2111,2112],{},"✅ Yes, 15 traditions",[670,2114,672],{},[646,2116,2117,2119,2122,2125,2128],{},[670,2118,1828],{},[670,2120,2121],{},"Fast sermon drafts",[670,2123,2124],{},"✅ Yes",[670,2126,2127],{},"➖ No",[670,2129,672],{},[646,2131,2132,2134,2137,2140,2142],{},[670,2133,1847],{},[670,2135,2136],{},"Verse study with memory",[670,2138,2139],{},"✅ Limited",[670,2141,2127],{},[670,2143,2144],{},"Freemium",[646,2146,2147,2149,2152,2154,2156],{},[670,2148,1871],{},[670,2150,2151],{},"Scripture chat, multilingual",[670,2153,2124],{},[670,2155,2127],{},[670,2157,672],{},[646,2159,2160,2162,2165,2167,2169],{},[670,2161,1890],{},[670,2163,2164],{},"Israelite tradition Q&A",[670,2166,2139],{},[670,2168,2127],{},[670,2170,2144],{},[646,2172,2173,2175,2178,2180,2182],{},[670,2174,1909],{},[670,2176,2177],{},"Faith questions, community",[670,2179,2124],{},[670,2181,2127],{},[670,2183,672],{},[646,2185,2186,2188,2191,2193,2195],{},[670,2187,1932],{},[670,2189,2190],{},"Occasion prayers",[670,2192,2124],{},[670,2194,2127],{},[670,2196,672],{},[646,2198,2199,2201,2204,2206,2208],{},[670,2200,1951],{},[670,2202,2203],{},"Christian dream journal",[670,2205,2124],{},[670,2207,2127],{},[670,2209,672],{},[646,2211,2212,2214,2217,2220,2222],{},[670,2213,1970],{},[670,2215,2216],{},"Emotion-led devotionals",[670,2218,2219],{},"⚠️ No",[670,2221,2127],{},[670,2223,2224],{},"Paid",[646,2226,2227,2229,2232,2234,2236],{},[670,2228,1997],{},[670,2230,2231],{},"Church management",[670,2233,2124],{},[670,2235,2127],{},[670,2237,2144],{},[646,2239,2240,2242,2245,2247,2249],{},[670,2241,2016],{},[670,2243,2244],{},"Church websites",[670,2246,2219],{},[670,2248,2127],{},[670,2250,2224],{},[646,2252,2253,2255,2258,2260,2262],{},[670,2254,2035],{},[670,2256,2257],{},"Worship set planning",[670,2259,2124],{},[670,2261,2127],{},[670,2263,672],{},[646,2265,2266,2268,2271,2273,2275],{},[670,2267,2058],{},[670,2269,2270],{},"Church discovery",[670,2272,2124],{},[670,2274,2127],{},[670,2276,672],{},[51,2278,2280],{"id":2279},"how-to-choose","How to Choose",[160,2282,2283,2292,2299,2306,2314,2321,2328,2339],{},[163,2284,2285,2288,2289,2291],{},[37,2286,2287],{},"\"I want one free tool that covers most of my week\""," → ",[37,2290,1787],{},", and set your denomination and translation on the first screen",[163,2293,2294,2288,2297],{},[37,2295,2296],{},"\"I just need a sermon draft by Friday\"",[37,2298,1828],{},[163,2300,2301,2288,2304],{},[37,2302,2303],{},"\"My study notes keep getting lost across apps\"",[37,2305,1847],{},[163,2307,2308,2288,2311,2313],{},[37,2309,2310],{},"\"I need the Greek and Hebrew, properly\"",[37,2312,1787],{},", the Strong's integration is the deepest here",[163,2315,2316,2288,2319],{},[37,2317,2318],{},"\"I need words for a funeral on Thursday\"",[37,2320,1932],{},[163,2322,2323,2288,2326],{},[37,2324,2325],{},"\"Our admin is a spreadsheet and it is breaking\"",[37,2327,1997],{},[163,2329,2330,2288,2333,2335,2336,2338],{},[37,2331,2332],{},"\"Nobody can find our church online\"",[37,2334,2016],{}," for the site, ",[37,2337,2058],{}," for the listing",[163,2340,2341,2288,2344],{},[37,2342,2343],{},"\"I plan worship and keep picking the same six songs\"",[37,2345,2035],{},[51,2347,2349],{"id":2348},"a-word-on-doctrine","A Word on Doctrine",[17,2351,2352,2353,2356],{},"One thing worth saying plainly: a general model will happily give you a Reformed answer on Monday and a Pentecostal one on Tuesday, because it has no reason to be consistent. That is the actual argument for church-specific AI, and it is why AlignedAI asking for your tradition ",[37,2354,2355],{},"before"," the first message matters more than any feature list.",[17,2358,2359],{},"Whatever you use, read the output before it reaches the pulpit. These tools are good at the first eighty percent. The last twenty is still yours.",[51,2361,784],{"id":783},[17,2363,2364,2365,2367,2368,2370,2371,2373],{},"The good news is that almost everything on this list is free to try, and the two strongest sermon tools cost nothing at all. Start with ",[37,2366,1787],{}," if you want one thing that does most of the job, add ",[37,2369,2035],{}," if you plan worship, and pick up ",[37,2372,1997],{}," when the admin gets heavy.",[17,2375,2376,2377,2381],{},"Browsing for more? See our full list of ",[21,2378,2380],{"href":2379},"\u002Fbest-products-by-tag\u002Fchristian","the best Christian apps"," on Uneed 🔥",{"title":870,"searchDepth":871,"depth":871,"links":2383},[2384,2389,2394,2399,2405,2406,2407,2408],{"id":1782,"depth":871,"text":1783,"children":2385},[2386,2387,2388],{"id":1786,"depth":877,"text":1787},{"id":1827,"depth":877,"text":1828},{"id":1846,"depth":877,"text":1847},{"id":1866,"depth":871,"text":1867,"children":2390},[2391,2392,2393],{"id":1870,"depth":877,"text":1871},{"id":1889,"depth":877,"text":1890},{"id":1908,"depth":877,"text":1909},{"id":1927,"depth":871,"text":1928,"children":2395},[2396,2397,2398],{"id":1931,"depth":877,"text":1932},{"id":1950,"depth":877,"text":1951},{"id":1969,"depth":877,"text":1970},{"id":1989,"depth":871,"text":1990,"children":2400},[2401,2402,2403,2404],{"id":1996,"depth":877,"text":1997},{"id":2015,"depth":877,"text":2016},{"id":2034,"depth":877,"text":2035},{"id":2057,"depth":877,"text":2058},{"id":2076,"depth":871,"text":2077},{"id":2279,"depth":871,"text":2280},{"id":2348,"depth":871,"text":2349},{"id":783,"depth":871,"text":784},"Tools","2026-09-04","The best AI tools for churches and pastors in 2026, from sermon prep and Bible study to prayer, worship planning and church admin. Free options included.",{},"\u002Fblog\u002Fbest-ai-tools-for-churches",{"title":1767,"description":2411},{"loc":2413},"blog\u002Fbest-ai-tools-for-churches","https:\u002F\u002Fbackend.uneed.best\u002Fstorage\u002Fv1\u002Fobject\u002Fpublic\u002Fblog\u002Fchurches-ai-tools.jpg","9CJaOSRNjfOC9bRQUfplEpcr0SkS76B4I5-N9Vz3kDk",{"ladder":2420,"premium":3467,"availability":3666,"seed":3667},[2421,2496,2547,2584,2619,2652,2681,2706,2728,2755,2780,2811,2832,2857,2887,2903,2927,2947,2969,2991,3011,3029,3045,3069,3092,3108,3127,3157,3185,3201,3221,3247,3272,3292,3307,3331,3353,3379,3399,3420,3439],{"id":2422,"name":2423,"slug":2424,"description":2425,"url":2426,"dofollow":892,"logo":2427,"